    {'path': 'documentation/prompts/RELAY_L456_gemini_addresses_sun_20261020.md', 'mode': 'new', 'new_md5': '272a73786efb25e4f4efdc67ff8bdffd', 'content': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Relay prompt for Gemini 4: find the open web address of each work the Sun room\'s citations name (L-456). Copy everything below the line into Gemini. -->\n# Relay prompt for Gemini 4 -- web addresses for the Sun room\'s citations (L-456)\n\nWritten 2026-10-20 by Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5 for Tony to carry to\nGemini 4. Copy everything below the line. Any effort tier will do: every\nrow names its work, so each is bounded (open it, find the sentence,\nstop). Claude re-opens every address that comes back before it goes on\na row. The Earth room\'s works are a second prompt, RELAY_L456_gemini_addresses_earth_20261020.md: two shorter answers lose less if the Gemini app drops one.\n\n---\n\nBuilt on palomas_orrery e8e714da99e0ec102fe179341cf767458d2e8545 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and\ntonyquintanilla.github.io ca5339fd3c22d61a1540e4314726e85003e48751 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io. Every\ncitation below is written out here as the website serves it today, so\nyou do not need the repositories.\nVocabulary: v2 (2026-08-13)\n\n## Who you are working for\n\nTony Quintanilla, PE, a retired civil and environmental engineer, builds\nPaloma\'s Orrery, a 3D astronomy visualization (a desktop program and the\nwebsite palomasorrery.com), through conversation with AI partners. He is\nnot a professional programmer or astronomer. Please write plainly.\n\n## The job\n\nThe website\'s information panel lists the sources behind each drawn\nfeature. Visitors should be able to tap a source and open it. These\ncitations name their work but carry no web address. For each WORK\nbelow, find the address of a copy anyone can read, open it, and show\nus that it is the same work and that it says what we cite it for.\n\n## The rules this job follows\n\nIf you have imported the palomas_orrery repository, read this file and\nname it at the top of your answer:\nskills/provenance-discipline/SKILL.md -- sections The Access Standard\nand What a Visitor Reads. If you have not imported it, say so; the\nrules you need are here:\n\n1. **Open copies first.** Prefer, in this order: the publisher\'s own\n   open-access page; an arXiv preprint of the same paper; the NASA ADS\n   abstract page; an agency\'s own page or document. A paywalled page is\n   still worth reporting as the DOI\'s address, but say it is paywalled\n   and keep looking for an open copy.\n2. **Name what you opened.** The title and authors exactly as printed\n   on the page you opened. A different paper with a similar title is a\n   different work: say so.\n3. **Quote it.** A short verbatim quote from the page you opened that\n   carries what we cite the work for, with a locator (section, table,\n   equation, page, or abstract). A row without both is recorded as\n   UNVERIFIED whatever it says. If you are recalling rather than\n   reading, say so and leave the row UNVERIFIED. Silence is better than\n   a guess.\n4. **Never build an address from memory.** Every address you report is\n   one you opened in this session.\n\n## Before any row: a pre-flight\n\nOpen this page and quote one sentence from it, so we know your fetching\nworks:\n\n```\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11533\n```\n\n## Your answer\'s header\n\nStart with one line naming your MODEL and your EFFORT TIER, then the\nrule file you read (or "not imported").\n\n## The table\n\nOne row per numbered work, in this exact layout:\n\n| # | Work as we cite it (short) | Address opened | Access word | Title and authors as printed | Quote | Locator | Same work? | Says what we cite? | Notes |\n\n- **Access word**: one of open full text, abstract, agency page, open\n  document, paywalled, book (no open copy).\n- Each verdict cell holds EXACTLY ONE token:\n\n```\nSame work?           YES  NO  UNVERIFIED\nSays what we cite?   YES  NO  PARTIAL  UNVERIFIED\n```\n\n- Where a work has a DOI, also give its doi.org address in Notes, even\n  when the open copy is elsewhere.\n- Put every address as a bare URL inside a code block after the table,\n  numbered by row. Links pasted as chips lose their address.\n- Please deliver the whole answer as a downloadable Markdown file named\n  worksheet_gemini4_<effort tier>_L456_sun_20261020.md, as well as in\n  the chat.\n\n## The works\n\nEach row gives the work as our citation names it, then, after "We cite\nit for:", the sentence or figure our citation rests on.\n\nS1. Delsemme (1987), A&A 187:913\nWe cite it for: the aphelia of 152 long-period comets avoid both galactic polar caps and a strip along the galactic equator.\n\nS2. Matese and Whitmire, arXiv:1004.4584, sec. 2.2\nWe cite it for: the galactic tide\'s main term goes as |sin B cos B|.\n\nS3. Higuchi (2020), AJ 160:134\nWe cite it for: a reading of the comets\' galactic-latitude pattern that differs from Delsemme\'s (row S1).\n\nS4. Gurnett, D. A., Kurth, W. S., Burlaga, L. F. and Ness, N. F. (2013), "In situ observations of interstellar plasma with Voyager 1", Science 341, 1489-1492, doi:10.1126/science.1241681\nWe cite it for: p. 1489: the first sign of the heliopause came on 28 July 2012, at 121 AU; the last of five crossings was on 25 August 2012.\n\nS5. IAU 2015 Resolution B3 (nominal solar radius), published as Prsa et al. (2016), AJ 152, 41, doi:10.3847/0004-6256/152/2/41\nWe cite it for: the nominal solar radius, 695,700 km. Report both the IAU\'s own page for the resolution, if one opens, and the paper.\n\nS6. IAU 2018 (Archinal et al.), the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements report\nWe cite it for: the Sun\'s north pole in ICRF equatorial coordinates (right ascension and declination of the pole).\n\nS7. Liu, Zhu and Hu, "Constructing a Galactic coordinate system based on near-infrared and radio catalogs", arXiv:1110.6268\nWe cite it for: eq. (2): the north galactic pole at J2000.0, alpha = 12h 51m 26.2755s, delta = +27 deg 07\' 41.704"; sec. 3.2, eq. (8): Sgr A* at alpha = 17h 45m 40.0400s, delta = -29 deg 00\' 28.138".\n\nS8. Murray, C. A. 1989, A&A 218, 325\nWe cite it for: the transformation to galactic coordinates adopted for Hipparcos (the paper Liu, Zhu and Hu draw the pole from).\n\nS9. ESA 1997, The Hipparcos and Tycho Catalogues, ESA SP-1200\nWe cite it for: the catalogue\'s adopted galactic pole (the volume Liu, Zhu and Hu cite as the standard).\n\nS10. Reid, M. J. and Brunthaler, A. 2004, ApJ 616, 872\nWe cite it for: the absolute position of Sgr A* from Very Long Baseline Array measurements.\n\nS11. NIST Special Publication 811, Guide for the Use of the SI, sec. 5.1.1, Table 6\nWe cite it for: the units accepted for use with the SI: the minute is 60 seconds, the hour 3600 seconds, and the second of arc one sixtieth of the minute of arc, which is one sixtieth of the degree.\n\nS12. Murray & Dermott, "Solar System Dynamics" (1999), Sec. 4.6\nWe cite it for: the Roche limit. This is a book: report a publisher\'s page or an open excerpt only if one opens; "book (no open copy)" is a fine answer.\n\nS13. NASA Science, "Oort Cloud Facts"\nWe cite it for: the inner edge of the Oort cloud is thought to lie between 2,000 and 5,000 AU from the Sun, and the outer edge between 10,000 and 100,000 AU.\n\nS14. Portegies Zwart, S., Torres, S., Cai, M. X. and Brown, A. G. A. (2021), "Oort cloud Ecology II: the chronology of the formation of the Oort cloud", A&A 652, A144, doi:10.1051/0004-6361/202040096\nWe cite it for: sec. 2.2: the Hills cloud lies between the outer edge of the parking zone and the inner edge of the Oort cloud, at about 20,000 au or less; the Sun\'s Hill radius in the Galactic potential is about 0.65 pc (Fig. 2 and Fig. 3 captions; sec. 5).\n\nS15. Stone, E. C., Cummings, A. C., McDonald, F. B., Heikkila, B. C., Lal, N. and Webber, W. R. (2005), "Voyager 1 explores the termination shock region and the heliosheath beyond", Science 309, 2017-2020, doi:10.1126/science.1117684\nWe cite it for: abstract: Voyager 1 crossed the termination shock on 16 December 2004 at 94.01 AU.\n\nS16. Suess & Nerney (2004), Adv. Space Res. 33:668-675, bibcode 2004AdSpR..33..668S\nWe cite it for: "the closed field regions, or helmets, reach no higher than 2-4 solar radii".\n\n\n## What not to do\n\n- Do not propose wording for the website.\n- Do not combine rows.\n- Do not fill a cell from memory. If you could not open a page, the\n  cell is UNVERIFIED and Notes says what stopped you.\n'},
    {'path': 'documentation/prompts/RELAY_L456_gemini_addresses_earth_20261020.md', 'mode': 'new', 'new_md5': '7195635bbd159c34bd1dbb7af9b9b7b3', 'content': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Relay prompt for Gemini 4: find the open web address of each work the Earth room\'s citations name (L-456). Copy everything below the line into Gemini. -->\n# Relay prompt for Gemini 4 -- web addresses for the Earth room\'s citations (L-456)\n\nWritten 2026-10-20 by Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5 for Tony to carry to\nGemini 4. Copy everything below the line. Any effort tier will do: every\nrow names its work, so each is bounded (open it, find the sentence,\nstop). Claude re-opens every address that comes back before it goes on\na row. The Sun room\'s works are a first prompt, RELAY_L456_gemini_addresses_sun_20261020.md: two shorter answers lose less if the Gemini app drops one.\n\n---\n\nBuilt on palomas_orrery e8e714da99e0ec102fe179341cf767458d2e8545 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and\ntonyquintanilla.github.io ca5339fd3c22d61a1540e4314726e85003e48751 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io. Every\ncitation below is written out here as the website serves it today, so\nyou do not need the repositories.\nVocabulary: v2 (2026-08-13)\n\n## Who you are working for\n\nTony Quintanilla, PE, a retired civil and environmental engineer, builds\nPaloma\'s Orrery, a 3D astronomy visualization (a desktop program and the\nwebsite palomasorrery.com), through conversation with AI partners. He is\nnot a professional programmer or astronomer. Please write plainly.\n\n## The job\n\nThe website\'s information panel lists the sources behind each drawn\nfeature. Visitors should be able to tap a source and open it. These\ncitations name their work but carry no web address. For each WORK\nbelow, find the address of a copy anyone can read, open it, and show\nus that it is the same work and that it says what we cite it for.\n\n## The rules this job follows\n\nIf you have imported the palomas_orrery repository, read this file and\nname it at the top of your answer:\nskills/provenance-discipline/SKILL.md -- sections The Access Standard\nand What a Visitor Reads. If you have not imported it, say so; the\nrules you need are here:\n\n1. **Open copies first.** Prefer, in this order: the publisher\'s own\n   open-access page; an arXiv preprint of the same paper; the NASA ADS\n   abstract page; an agency\'s own page or document. A paywalled page is\n   still worth reporting as the DOI\'s address, but say it is paywalled\n   and keep looking for an open copy.\n2. **Name what you opened.** The title and authors exactly as printed\n   on the page you opened. A different paper with a similar title is a\n   different work: say so.\n3. **Quote it.** A short verbatim quote from the page you opened that\n   carries what we cite the work for, with a locator (section, table,\n   equation, page, or abstract). A row without both is recorded as\n   UNVERIFIED whatever it says. If you are recalling rather than\n   reading, say so and leave the row UNVERIFIED. Silence is better than\n   a guess.\n4. **Never build an address from memory.** Every address you report is\n   one you opened in this session.\n\n## Before any row: a pre-flight\n\nOpen this page and quote one sentence from it, so we know your fetching\nworks:\n\n```\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11533\n```\n\n## Your answer\'s header\n\nStart with one line naming your MODEL and your EFFORT TIER, then the\nrule file you read (or "not imported").\n\n## The table\n\nOne row per numbered work, in this exact layout:\n\n| # | Work as we cite it (short) | Address opened | Access word | Title and authors as printed | Quote | Locator | Same work? | Says what we cite? | Notes |\n\n- **Access word**: one of open full text, abstract, agency page, open\n  document, paywalled, book (no open copy).\n- Each verdict cell holds EXACTLY ONE token:\n\n```\nSame work?           YES  NO  UNVERIFIED\nSays what we cite?   YES  NO  PARTIAL  UNVERIFIED\n```\n\n- Where a work has a DOI, also give its doi.org address in Notes, even\n  when the open copy is elsewhere.\n- Put every address as a bare URL inside a code block after the table,\n  numbered by row. Links pasted as chips lose their address.\n- Please deliver the whole answer as a downloadable Markdown file named\n  worksheet_gemini4_<effort tier>_L456_earth_20261020.md, as well as in\n  the chat.\n\n## The works\n\nEach row gives the work as our citation names it, then, after "We cite\nit for:", the sentence or figure our citation rests on.\n\nE1. Alken, P. et al. (2021), "International Geomagnetic Reference Field: the thirteenth generation", Earth Planets Space 73:49, doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x\nWe cite it for: Table 4: the 2020.0 north geomagnetic pole at 80.65 N, -72.68 E.\n\nE2. IGRF-13 coefficient file igrf13coeffs.txt (IAGA Working Group V-MOD, served by NOAA NCEI)\nWe cite it for: g(1,0) at epoch 2020.0 is -29404.8 nT. Report the file\'s own address, opened.\n\nE3. Baker, D. N. et al. (2018), "Space Weather Effects in the Earth\'s Radiation Belts", Space Sci. Rev. 214:17, doi:10.1007/s11214-017-0452-7\nWe cite it for: sec. 2: inner-zone proton fluxes peak near geocentric r ~ 1.5 R_E.\n\nE4. Baliukin, I. I., Bertaux, J.-L., Quemerais, E., Izmodenov, V. V. & Schmidt, W. (2019), "SWAN/SOHO Lyman-alpha mapping: the hydrogen geocorona extends well beyond the Moon", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 124, 861-885, doi:10.1029/2018JA026136\nWe cite it for: the geocorona is detected to at least 100 Earth radii.\n\nE5. Dziewonski, A. M. & Anderson, D. L. (1981), "Preliminary reference Earth model", Phys. Earth Planet. Inter. 25:297-356\nWe cite it for: the inner core boundary at r = 1221.5 km, the core-mantle boundary at r = 3480 km, and the base of the crust at r = 6346.6 km.\n\nE6. Folkner, W. M., Williams, J. G., Boggs, D. H., Park, R. S. & Kuchynka, P. (2014), "The Planetary and Lunar Ephemerides DE430 and DE431", IPN Progress Report 42-196\nWe cite it for: abstract: the inner planets\' orbits are known to subkilometer accuracy.\n\nE7. IADC Space Debris Mitigation Guidelines, IADC-02-01 REVISION 2, March 2020\nWe cite it for: section 3.3.2 (1): the LEO Protected Region extends from the Earth\'s surface to an altitude of 2,000 km; section 3.3.1 takes the equatorial radius, 6,378 km, as the reference surface.\n\nE8. IAU 2012 Resolution B2\nWe cite it for: the astronomical unit\'s exact definition.\n\nE9. IERS Conventions (2010), Petit & Luzum (eds.), IERS Technical Note No. 36\nWe cite it for: Table 1.1: the Earth\'s equatorial radius and the geocentric gravitational constant GM_E = 3.986004418e14 m^3 s^-2; Table 1.2: the mean Earth angular velocity 7.292115e-5 rad s^-1; sec. 2.1.1: the mean pole at J2000.0 within about 0.02 arcseconds of the ICRS pole.\n\nE10. Jelinek, K., Nemecek, Z. and Safrankova, J. (2012), "A new approach to magnetopause and bow shock modeling based on automated region identification", J. Geophys. Res. 117, A05208, doi:10.1029/2011JA017252\nWe cite it for: eq. 14, p. 5: R_BS = 15.02 p^(-1/6.55); sec. 4: lambda = 1.17 for the bow shock; fig. 7: a scatter of 0.69 R_E.\n\nE11. Li, X., Xiang, Z., Mei, Y., O\'Brien, D., Brennan, D., Zhao, H., Baker, D. N. and Temerin, M. A. (2025), "A New Electron and Proton Radiation Belt Identified by CIRBE/REPTile-2 Measurements After the Magnetic Super Storm of 10 May 2024", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 130, e2024JA033504, doi:10.1029/2024JA033504\nWe cite it for: sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5.\n\nE12. Meredith et al. (2014), doi:10.1002/2014JA020064\nWe cite it for: introduction: the span of the inner and outer belts, the outer belt extending to 7 Earth radii.\n\nE13. Li, Tu et al. (2024), doi:10.1029/2023JA032171\nWe cite it for: introduction: the outer belt extending to 7 Earth radii.\n\nE14. NASA Planetary Fact Sheet, Earth\nWe cite it for: the volumetric mean radius.\n\nE15. Ishii, T., Huang, R., Fei, H. et al. (2018), "Complete agreement of the post-spinel transition with the 660-km seismic discontinuity", Sci. Rep. 8:6358, doi:10.1038/s41598-018-24832-y\nWe cite it for: the global average depth of the discontinuity is 660 +/- 10 km.\n\nE16. NOAA JetStream, "Layers of the Atmosphere"\nWe cite it for: the stratosphere to about 50 km; the thermosphere from about 85 km to about 600 km, the exosphere beyond.\n\nE17. NASA, "Earth\'s Atmospheric Layers"\nWe cite it for: the stratosphere extends to 50 km; the thermosphere extends to 600 km.\n\nE18. Shue, J.-H., Song, P., Russell, C. T. et al. (1998), "Magnetopause location under extreme solar wind conditions", J. Geophys. Res. 103(A8), 17691-17700, doi:10.1029/98JA01103\nWe cite it for: Table 1 "After Fit", p. 17,698: a1 = 10.22 +/- 0.10 R_E; p. 17,697: the model\'s standard deviation against the crossings, 1.23 R_E.\n\nE19. Slavin, J. A., Smith, E. J., Sibeck, D. G., Baker, D. N., Zwickl, R. D. and Akasofu, S.-I. (1985), "An ISEE 3 study of average and substorm conditions in the distant magnetotail", J. Geophys. Res. 90(A11), 10875-10895, doi:10.1029/JA090iA11p10875\nWe cite it for: abstract: flaring ceases on average at |X| = 120 +/- 10 R_E; a magnetotail diameter of 60 +/- 5 R_E at |X| = 130 - 225 R_E.\n\nE20. Slavin et al. (1983), Geophys. Res. Lett. 10(10):973, doi:10.1029/GL010i010p00973\nWe cite it for: the magnetotail observed to 220 R_E.\n\nE21. Sibeck and Lin (2014), J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 119, doi:10.1002/2013JA019471\nWe cite it for: the distant tail is often flattened along the part of the solar wind\'s magnetic field that lies across the Sun-Earth line.\n\nE22. Maezawa, Hori, Mukai, Saito, Yamamoto, Kokubun and Nishida (1997), Adv. Space Res. 20(4-5):949, doi:10.1016/S0273-1177(97)00503-6\nWe cite it for: GEOTAIL found the distant tail about the same size in both directions under average conditions.\n\nE23. JPL Horizons manual, its reference frames section\nWe cite it for: the ICRF was built to align with FK5/J2000 and differs from it by at most 0.02 arcseconds.\n\n\n## What not to do\n\n- Do not propose wording for the website.\n- Do not combine rows.\n- Do not fill a cell from memory. If you could not open a page, the\n  cell is UNVERIFIED and Notes says what stopped you.\n'},
    {'path': 'documentation/HANDOFF_L456_source_list_20261020.md', 'mode': 'new', 'new_md5': 'd78fabceded2a076e36b3f092757ac8d', 'content': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Session record of L-456\'s first build: the i panel\'s sources as a linked list in every room, Earth\'s four inner shells in Earth radii, the Gemini prompts for the missing addresses, and the rulings on a monthly run and a "report a problem" link; two patches, with Tony\'s run order. -->\n# Handoff: the i panel\'s sources as a linked list (L-456)\n\nBuilt on orrery e8e714da99e0ec102fe179341cf767458d2e8545 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery ("L410 sun fades\nclose and cleanup") and gallery ca5339fd3c22d61a1540e4314726e85003e48751\nat https://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io ("L 410\nfixture hovers"). Both patches of section 1 are built on those two SHAs;\nneither is run yet.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\nType: BUILD, with rulings. Written October 20, 2026 by Anthropic\'s\nClaude Opus 5.5, the same session as\n`HANDOFF_L410_sun_fades_build_20261020.md`, which it follows. Your\nverdicts are in your run record, `WHERE_WE_ARE_10-18-26_1326_run_record.md`,\nthe session of 10-20.\n\n## 1. Your run order (every Tony-action of this session, in one list)\n\nEach patch checks the files it edits and refuses rather than guess. The\nwebsite patch goes FIRST: the orrery patch gives the dashboard a button\nfor the website\'s new check, and the dashboard\'s own check fails while\nthe website lacks it.\n\n1. **(do)** Gallery folder: run `patch_L456_1_source_list_website_20261020.py`.\n2. **(do)** Move it into the gallery\'s `documentation/`, and\n   `patch_L410_7_sun_fades_website_20261020.py` with it, which is still\n   in the gallery\'s top folder (pushed there at ca5339fd). Both before\n   the Daily Run, which would otherwise list them in `module_atlas.py`\n   as modules.\n3. **(do)** The Daily Run. Expect 31 of 31 gating, "Source list" PASS,\n   "Display figures" PASS. Its Config mirror writes the Alfven\n   surface\'s new citation, with its address, from the orrery. Then\n   commit and push.\n4. **(do)** The live check: `python gallery_maintenance_run.py --live`.\n5. **(look)** Mode 5 on your phone: the Sun room, Corona, tap the i: a\n   bulleted list of sources, each address a link; tap one. Then the\n   Earth room, the Inner Core\'s hover: "Radius: 0.19151 Earth radii",\n   then "= 1,221.5 km (0.000008165 AU)".\n6. **(do)** Orrery folder: run `patch_L456_2_session_close_20261020.py`,\n   then move it into `documentation/`.\n7. **(do)** The orrery maintenance run. Expect 31 of 31 gating, the gate\n   line 0 TIER-1 (whole tree 280), "Skill headers" OK and "Dashboard\n   parity" PASS. It rewrites the ledger\'s index and the Skill Manifest\n   (interactive-exhibit 1.18). Commit; push.\n8. **(do)** Reinstall interactive-exhibit (1.18) in Settings > Skills.\n9. **(do)** Carry the two Gemini prompts, in the orrery\'s\n   `documentation/prompts/`, to Gemini 4: `RELAY_L456_gemini_addresses_sun_20261020.md`\n   and `RELAY_L456_gemini_addresses_earth_20261020.md`. A desktop\n   browser may hold up better than the phone app. Save each answer in\n   `documentation/worksheets/` under the name the prompt asks for.\n\n## 2. What was built\n\n**The website, patch_L456_1** (gallery folder):\n- `gallery/source_list.js` (new): turns a feature\'s citations into one\n  list, and finds where each web address in a citation ends.\n- `interactive.html`: loads it. The i panel prints "Source:" or\n  "Sources:" and a bulleted list: each citation after its label in\n  bold, the website\'s note after a dash, every address a link that\n  opens in a new tab, long addresses wrapping on the phone. Every room.\n- `gallery/feature_renderers.js`: where citations are gathered (the\n  corona, the dust cloud, a shell whose words name a second number, the\n  exosphere\'s fade) they also ride as a list, `source_items`. Earth\'s\n  inner core, outer core, lower mantle and upper mantle give their\n  radius in Earth radii first, from the row\'s served "in".\n- `gallery/earth_geometry.js`: the axis\'s, the Sun direction\'s, the\n  terminator\'s and the Moon arc\'s citations as a list too.\n- `documentation/smoke_source_list.js` (new, gating "Source list"):\n  names, for every feature in the Sun room and Earth\'s recorded scene,\n  how many citations it lists and how many carry a link.\n- `documentation/smoke_display_figures.js`: grades the four new\n  Earth-radii lines by its own arithmetic and pins your approved\n  figures. The hover fixture did not change.\n- `gallery_maintenance_run.py`: runs the new check; the live check\n  fetches `gallery/source_list.js`.\n\nThe joined citation line that every other check reads is unchanged.\n\n**The orrery, patch_L456_2** (the close):\n- `palomas_orrery_dashboard.py`: a Source List button for the website\'s\n  new check; and "Horizons Check -- everything, now" in Tools and\n  Caches, `horizons_check.py --all`, which confirms every object and\n  data sheet today rather than only what is due.\n- interactive-exhibit 1.18 (section 5); protocol v3.106; the ledger;\n  Where We Are; this handoff; the two Gemini prompts, already in your\n  folder.\n\nHow many citations carry a link, measured at ca5339fd: the Sun room 22\nof 43, the Earth room 9 of 29. The addresses close that gap.\n\n## 3. Your rulings this session\n\n- From the phone: "need hyperlinks" and "need some kind of itemization\n  for readability". Then: "can we do L456 in this session so we are\n  ready for the sun\'s 14 pairs?" So L-456 goes before L-191\'s fourteen\n  pairs.\n- The addresses: "ask Gemini to do the search and Claude checks they\n  are correct."\n- The link check: every 30 days, automatically, "Yes"; and by hand any\n  time. "Can you add the 30-day Checkers to the dashboard?" -- the\n  Horizons button now; the link check\'s with its build.\n- One monthly run, started by the Daily Run, with the link check as its\n  first job: "Yes, as recommended." (L-459)\n- A "report a problem" link: reports land "in the daily maintenance\n  report"; through the guest book\'s form, pre-filled, no second sheet;\n  for any problem a visitor sees, "perhaps an expert would see a\n  problem we have not caught." "Confirmed." (L-458)\n\n## 4. How it was checked\n\n- The website patch, on clean copies at ca5339fd with Unix and with\n  Windows line endings: the tested bytes both times; a second run\n  refuses.\n- The website\'s maintenance run on a stand-in copy: 31 of 31 gating\n  (the three pulls, and the two mirrors that wait on them, fail only\n  because the sandbox cannot reach GitHub\'s API; they are not gating). "Source list" breaks its own file two ways first and goes red\n  both times. "Hover budget", "Typed facts", "Typed numbers" and\n  "Display figures" pass.\n- The corona\'s i panel drawn headless at phone width with the page\'s\n  own panel code and styles (`panel_preview_corona.png`, sent in chat).\n  The phone has not seen it: step 5.\n- The orrery close: the dashboard compiles; "Dashboard parity" passes\n  against the patched gallery, 10 of 10 planted faults caught.\n\n## 5. Skill versions and what travels\n\nThis session\'s loaded copies read interactive-exhibit 1.17 and\nprovenance-cross-check 1.1, as the manifest expected, beside the\nearlier half\'s (`HANDOFF_L410_sun_fades_build_20261020.md`, section 5).\ninteractive-exhibit goes to 1.18 in patch_L456_2; the next session\nconfirms its loaded copy reads 1.18 before exhibit work, and\nprovenance-discipline 2.32 before provenance work.\n\n## 6. What is left, and the next session\n\n- The addresses: when Gemini\'s two answers are saved, re-open every\n  address before it goes on its row\'s Source line in the orrery\n  (provenance-discipline 2.32), the Sun\'s first, then Earth\'s; a review\n  of each return. Then the website\'s Daily Run carries them.\n- Then L-191: the Sun\'s fourteen hover and tooltip pairs reconciled\n  (its entry records what was measured).\n- Still on L-456: the Celsius words for Earth\'s surface and air, as a\n  list for you; the export serving a derived row\'s inherited citations\n  as a list, so they itemize too.\n- L-459 with L-266: the monthly run and its link check, after the\n  addresses are in. L-458: the "report a problem" link, when its words\n  list is ready.\n'},
    {'path': 'LEDGER_CONSOLIDATED.md', 'mode': 'edit', 'base_md5': 'fa55bd6c4bd8d050e6260577e2f869aa', 'new_md5': '1d8f6a529c40f0df575ebfa6aa5d2eb3', 'zone': ('<!-- INDEX:START', '<!-- INDEX:END -->'), 'edits': [('fcd191e9 and gallery e8f6dd5e.\nReview and RICE update Tony 6-21-2026\n', 'fcd191e9 and gallery e8f6dd5e.\nModule updated: October 20, 2026, evening, with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus\n5.5 (L-456 parts 1, 3 and 4 built: the i panel\'s sources as a list with\nlive links, Earth\'s four inner shells in Earth radii; the Gemini prompts\nfor the missing addresses; L-458 new, a "report a problem" link through\nthe guest book\'s form; L-459 new, a monthly run started by the Daily\nRun; L-266\'s 30 days and its home; interactive-exhibit 1.18), built on\ne8e714da and gallery ca5339fd.\nReview and RICE update Tony 6-21-2026\n'), ("\n#### [L-456] What a visitor reads, from Earth's Mode 5 look: body radii, sources as a list with live links, Celsius for Earth's surface and air (gallery + orrery)\n", '\n#### [L-459] A monthly run for slow checks, started by the Daily Run (gallery + orrery, maintenance)\n<!-- L:459 status:OPEN upd:2026-10-20 section:A flag: rice: -->\n- **Tony, 2026-10-20**, in a design chat on verification: "add a monthly\n  run for monthly checks like this in addition to the daily run."\n  Brought to this session as a draft; Claude\'s recommendation, one\n  monthly run rather than a 30-day counter in each check, "Yes, as\n  recommended."\n- **Started by the Daily Run, never remembered** (A Check That Cannot\n  Fail Is Not Passing: put the check where it runs). The run keeps the\n  date of its last run in a file. Each Daily Run reads it and starts the\n  monthly run once 30 days or more have passed, so a missed day or a\n  month away still fires on the next run. On other days it prints one\n  line: when the monthly run last ran and when it is next due, so a\n  monthly run that stopped happening shows as an old date.\n- **Also run by hand, any time** (Tony: "Can I run the checker at\n  another time optionally?"): a dashboard button, "Monthly Run -- now",\n  runs everything in it at once and resets the date. Tony asked for the\n  30-day checks on the dashboard; the Horizons Check\'s "everything,\n  now" button was added the same evening (L-266).\n- **First job: L-266, the link check.** Others join when each is ruled\n  on.\n- **The Horizons Check is not moved into it.** It already keeps its own\n  dates for each object and data sheet, asks again from day 25, and\n  runs in every maintenance run asking JPL nothing on most days.\n**Gap:** build it with L-266\'s link check: where the date file lives,\nwhich repository\'s Daily Run starts it, how its findings reach the run\nrecord.\n**Ref:** L-266 (its first job); L-395 (the Horizons Check\'s own dates);\nL-446 (the Daily Run\'s order); L-416 (a dashboard button per step).\n\n#### [L-458] A "report a problem" link in every room\'s i panel (gallery, interactive)\n<!-- L:458 status:OPEN upd:2026-10-20 section:A flag: rice: -->\n- **Tony\'s idea, 2026-10-20**, in a design chat on verification: the\n  live source links in the i panel let a visitor check a claim, and "if\n  they see something off, they could report it. You know, that could be\n  me, or that could be somebody else." Brought to this session as a\n  draft.\n- **For any problem, not only a dead link** (Tony: "In fact the problem\n  report could report not just a broken link but any problem the\n  visitor sees, perhaps an expert would see a problem we have not\n  caught."). Dead links are the monthly run\'s (L-459); this is the\n  outside check only a visitor can make: a number, a sentence, a\n  source, a link or the drawing.\n- **Where it lands: Tony\'s Daily Run report** (Tony: "in the daily\n  maintenance report").\n- **How: the guest book\'s form, pre-filled** (Tony: "instead of\n  creating a second sheet couldn\'t have the function preformat a\n  message for the visitor to send or amend as a comment?"; then\n  "Confirmed"). The link opens the guest book\'s Google Form with its\n  message box already written, naming the room and the feature, for\n  the visitor to edit and send. It lands in the guest book\'s sheet; the\n  Daily Run\'s guest book step shows it to Tony. A message beginning\n  "Problem report" is shown as a report, kept private, and never offered\n  for the lobby. No second form, no second sheet, and the visitor needs\n  no account. First draft of the message, for Tony\'s words list at the\n  build: "Problem report -- Sun room, Corona. What looks wrong (a\n  number, a sentence, a source, a link, or the drawing)? If you know a\n  better source, please name it:"\n- **Tony-action (do), at the build:** in the guest book form\'s editor,\n  "Get pre-filled link" once, so the link knows the hidden name of the\n  message box. Steps given then.\n**Gap:** the build: the link in the shared i-panel chrome, so every\nroom has it at once (interactive-exhibit); the guest book updater\ntelling a report from a greeting; the message\'s words as a list for\nTony first.\n**Ref:** L-456 (the panel\'s sources as a list with links); L-281 (the\nguest book: its form, sheet and updater); L-265 (the i panel\'s links);\nL-459 (the monthly run).\n\n#### [L-456] What a visitor reads, from Earth\'s Mode 5 look: body radii, sources as a list with live links, Celsius for Earth\'s surface and air (gallery + orrery)\n'), ("interactive-exhibit's bump.\n**Ref:** gallery `gallery/feature_renderers.js`, `interactive.html`;\nprovenance-discipline (The Unit Field); interactive-exhibit; L-455,\nL-421, L-385.\n\n", 'interactive-exhibit\'s bump.\n- **2026-10-20, Tony moved this item ahead of L-191\'s reconciliation**,\nfrom his phone, with a screenshot of the corona\'s i panel: "As you can\n  see the source info is very hard to read: 1) need hyperlinks 2) need\n  some kind of itemization for readability." Then: "can we do L456 in\n  this session so we are ready for the sun\'s 14 pairs?" And on the\n  addresses: "ask Gemini to do the search and Claude checks they are\n  correct."\n- **Built 2026-10-20, parts (1), (3) and (4)** (Claude Opus 5.5; record\n  `documentation/HANDOFF_L456_source_list_20261020.md`; gallery patch\n  `patch_L456_1_source_list_website_20261020.py`, built on ca5339fd):\n  - (3) and (4): the i panel prints "Sources:" and a bulleted list, each\n    citation after its label, every web address a link that opens in a\n    new tab, long addresses wrapping. New `gallery/source_list.js`\n    decides what the list holds and where an address ends. The\n    renderers serve gathered citations as a list, `source_items`,\n    beside the joined `source` line, which is unchanged. The ".; " goes\n    with the joining. Every room at once.\n  - (1): Earth\'s inner core, outer core, lower mantle and upper mantle\n    give "Radius: 0.19151 Earth radii" (0.54561, 0.895, 0.99506) and then\n    "= 1,221.5 km (0.000008165 AU)" and so on, from each row\'s served\n    "in", as approved on 2026-10-18.\n  - The gallery run\'s new gating check, "Source list"\n    (`documentation/smoke_source_list.js`), names for every feature in\n    the Sun room and Earth\'s recorded scene how many citations it lists\n    and how many carry a link: at ca5339fd, the Sun 22 of 43, Earth 9 of\n    29. The orrery\'s dashboard gets its button.\n  - interactive-exhibit 1.18 (protocol v3.106).\n- **The addresses, part of the 98 class:** in the Sun and Earth rooms,\n  59 different citation texts carry no web address (18 the Sun\'s, 41\n  Earth\'s), naming 39 works. Two Gemini prompts went to Tony,\n  `documentation/prompts/RELAY_L456_gemini_addresses_sun_20261020.md`\n  (16 works) and `..._earth_20261020.md` (23), split so a dropped answer\n  costs less. Each address that comes back is opened again by Claude\n  before it goes on its row\'s Source line in the orrery.\n- **Found while building:** a citation the orrery\'s export builds from\n  other rows\' citations (a derived row\'s `source_inherited`, Earth\'s\n  Hill sphere for one) arrives as one joined line and stays one bullet.\n  Splitting it safely needs the export to serve the inherited citations\n  as a list. One class; recorded, not chased.\n**Gap (replaces the one above, 2026-10-20):** Tony\'s run of the gallery\npatch, the Daily Run and his look on the phone; the addresses, from\nGemini\'s two answers, each re-opened, onto their rows in the orrery,\nthe Sun\'s and Earth\'s first; the Celsius words for Earth\'s surface and\nair as a list for Tony; the export serving inherited citations as a\nlist. Then L-191\'s reconciliation of the Sun\'s fourteen pairs.\n**Ref:** gallery `gallery/feature_renderers.js`, `gallery/source_list.js`,\n`interactive.html`; provenance-discipline (The Unit Field, 2.32);\ninteractive-exhibit 1.18; L-455, L-421, L-385, L-458, L-266.\n\n'), ("**Gap (replaces the one above, 2026-10-20):** the website half of\nL-410 with L-131 (Tony's runs); the next session reconciles the Sun's\nfourteen hover and tooltip pairs (L-191; Tony's ruling of 2026-10-20),\nand L-456 comes after that; the streamer belt's and the clumps'\neyeballed numbers; L-241; then L-443, with L-128 after it.\n", "**Gap (replaces the one above, 2026-10-20):** the website half of\nL-410 with L-131 (Tony's runs); L-456 first, by Tony's word that\nevening (the panel built; the addresses next), then the Sun's fourteen\nhover and tooltip pairs (L-191); the streamer belt's and the clumps'\neyeballed numbers; L-241; then L-443, with L-128 after it.\n"), ("  These two differences from the finding as given went to Tony.\n**Gap (rewritten 2026-10-18):** (0) done, by Tony's look. (1a) BUILT:\n", '  These two differences from the finding as given went to Tony.\n- **2026-10-20, later:** Tony moved L-456\'s panel work ahead of this\n  reconciliation ("can we do L456 in this session so we are ready for\n  the sun\'s 14 pairs?"); it was built that evening. The fourteen pairs\n  are next after L-456\'s addresses.\n**Gap (rewritten 2026-10-18):** (0) done, by Tony\'s look. (1a) BUILT:\n'), ('  interval is his to choose.\n- **Ref:** L-265 (what makes it urgent); L-236 (the gallery runner and\n  its three-state convention); L-235 (checks that cannot fail,\n  gallery side); L-456 (the live links); provenance-discipline 2.8, The Access Standard --\n  reachability is already a stated precondition of a citation, with\n', '  interval is his to choose.\n- **Ruled 2026-10-20: every 30 days, automatically, and by hand any\n  time.** Tony asked for a recommendation; Claude recommended 30 days,\n  the Horizons Check\'s rhythm, a dead link visible to visitors for at\n  most a month, and a minute or two once a month. Tony: "Yes." Then:\n  "to confirm then 30-day counter is automatic and the checker fires\n  automatically? Can I run the checker at another time optionally?" --\n  yes to both. Then "Can you add the 30-day Checkers to the dashboard?"\n- **Its home is the monthly run, L-459** (Tony: "Yes, as recommended"),\n  started by the Daily Run when 30 days have passed, with a "now"\n  button on the dashboard that runs it at once and resets the date. It\n  runs on Tony\'s machine: the sandbox cannot reach most of these sites.\n  So shape (b) above, the gallery\'s maintenance run, reading both\n  repositories\' links, since the scale (about 470 addresses in the\n  orrery, and the website\'s) is why it belongs in a monthly run.\n- **States, as above, with one change:** a link that fails is tried\n  once more a few seconds later in the same run, and only a link that\n  fails both times is reported dead, by name. A redirect is reported\n  with where it now goes. A site that cannot be reached is counted\n  apart and does not fail the run.\n- **Built the same evening, its neighbour:** the dashboard\'s "Horizons\n  Check -- everything, now" button (`horizons_check.py --all`), in\n  Tools and Caches.\n- **Ref:** L-265 (what makes it urgent); L-236 (the gallery runner and\n  its three-state convention); L-235 (checks that cannot fail,\n  gallery side); L-456 (the live links); L-459 (the monthly run, its\n  home); L-458 (the visitor\'s report); provenance-discipline 2.8, The\n  Access Standard --\n  reachability is already a stated precondition of a citation, with\n')], 'anchors_only': True},
